notes:
Chainguard, OpenSourceMalware and Orca published their own analyses. The OpenSSF malicious-package advisory, mirrored in the GitHub Advisory Database, says it has not attributed this to a known campaign.

The Register carries Microsoft's statement that it has made some repositories private while it investigates malicious content. The Hacker News identifies those repositories, and BleepingComputer reported on the incident.

The page also cites the PyPA advisory database pull request, Wiz's post on the durabletask compromise of May 2026 as context, and the MITRE pages for six of its techniques. They are left off these slides for space.

The background slides name their own sources, MITRE ATT&CK and Microsoft Learn.

== Related kill chains
layout: links
link: Storm-0558, a Microsoft signing key | breaches/storm-0558.html
link: LiteLLM / TeamPCP | breaches/litellm-pypi-teampcp.html
link: LiteLLM 1.107.0 | breaches/litellm-1-107-release-hijack.html
link: Mini Shai-Hulud | breaches/mini-shai-hulud-tanstack.html
link: 3CX | breaches/3cx-x-trader.html
link: Ultralytics | breaches/ultralytics-cache-poisoning.html
link: Codecov | breaches/codecov-bash-uploader.html
The page calls Storm-0558 the closest chain on the site.
notes:
The page links seven other kill chains.

It calls Storm-0558 the closest chain on this site: a Microsoft signing key was used to forge access tokens, and the Cyber Safety Review Board concluded that Microsoft does not know how the key was obtained.

The same .pth startup trick carried the LiteLLM / TeamPCP and LiteLLM 1.107.0 payloads, and LiteLLM / TeamPCP also had no matching tag for its release. GitHub Actions OIDC tokens were also the target in Mini Shai-Hulud, there scraped from runner memory to publish packages.

In 3CX the attacker had to reach the vendor's build environments. Ultralytics and LiteLLM 1.107.0 show that attestations do not stop a release that comes through the official path. Codecov is the older lesson of an artifact swapped outside the build.

The page's related defense topics are CI/CD security, GitHub Actions security, Azure security, non-human identity, AI/ML security and incident response.
